{{also|Appendix:Variations of "cane"}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|cane}}, {{m|enm|canne}}, from {{der|en|fro|cane|t=sugar cane}}, from {{der|en|la|canna|t=reed}}, from {{der|en|grc|κάννα}}, from {{der|en|akk|𒄀|t=reed|tr=qanû}}, from {{der|en|sux|𒄀𒈾|tr=gi.na}}. {{doublet|en|canna|kaneh}}. Related to {{m|en|channel}} and {{m|en|canal}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/keɪn/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-cane.ogg|a=US|IPA=[kʰẽːn]}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪn|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|Cain}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[plant]] with simple [[stems]], like [[bamboo]] or [[sugar cane]], or the stem thereof
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} The slender, flexible main [[stem]] of a plant such as [[bamboo]], including many species in the grass family {{taxfmt|Gramineae|family}}
##: {{syn|en|stem|stalk|q3=of a tree|trunk}}
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} The plant itself, including many species in the grass family {{taxfmt|Gramineae|family}}; a [[reed]]
##: {{syn|en|reed}}
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[sugar cane|Sugar cane]]
##: {{syn|en|molasses cane}}
##* {{RQ:Bindloss Dust of Conflict|chapter=7|passage=Still, a dozen men with rifles, and cartridges to match, stayed behind when they filed through a white aldea lying silent amid the '''cane''', and the Sin Verguenza swung into slightly quicker stride.}}
## {{lb|en|US|Southern}} [[maize|Maize]] or, rarely, [[sorghum]], when such plants are processed to make [[molasses]] ([[treacle]]) or sugar
# The stem of such a plant adapted for use as a tool
## {{lb|en|countable}} A short [[rod]] or [[stick]], traditionally of wood or bamboo, used for [[corporal punishment]].
##: {{syn|en|rod|switch}}
##* {{RQ:Lindsay Redheap|page=123|passage=He stalked behind her simple narrative, a kill-joy parent, hasty, intolerant, keeping a special '''cane''' to enforce the authority of his sadistic God[.]}}
## {{lb|en|with "the"}} Corporal punishment by beating with a cane.
##: {{syn|en|a [[caning]]|six of the best|whipping|cuts}}
##: {{ux|en|The teacher gave his student the '''cane''' for throwing paper.}}
## A lance or dart made of cane
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1670|author=w:John Dryden|title=w:The Conquest of Granada
|passage=Judgelike thou sitt'st, to praise or to arraign / The flying skirmish of the darted '''cane'''.}}
# A rod-shaped tool or device, resembling the stem of the plant.
## {{lb|en|countable}} A strong short [[staff]] used for support or decoration during walking; a [[walking stick]]
##: {{syn|en|staff|walking stick}}
##: {{ux|en|After breaking his leg, he needed a '''cane''' to walk.}}
##* {{RQ:Orczy Miss Elliott|chapter=The Ayrsham Mystery|passage=The '''cane''' was undoubtedly of foreign make, for it had a solid silver ferrule at one end, which was not English hall–marked.}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=10|passage=Men that I knew around Wapatomac didn't wear high, shiny plug hats, nor yeller spring overcoats, nor carry '''canes''' with ivory heads as big as a catboat's anchor, as you might say.}}
## {{lb|en|countable|glassblowing}} A length of colored and/or patterned glass rod, used in the specific [[glassblowing]] technique called [[caneworking]]
## {{lb|en|countable}} A long [[rod]] often [[collapsible]] and commonly white (for visibility to other persons), used by [[vision impaired]] persons for guidance in determining their [[course]] and for probing for [[obstacle]]s in their path
##: {{syn|en|blind man's cane|white cane}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[split|Split]] [[rattan]], as used in [[wickerwork]] and [[basketry]].
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=1|passage=The bed was the most extravagant piece. Its graceful '''cane''' halftester rose high towards the cornice and was so festooned in carved white wood that the effect was positively insecure, as if the great couch were trimmed with icing sugar.}}
# A local European measure of length; the [[canna]].

